Root-cause hypotheses
H1 · The contact form's submit handler was never wired to a backend
High
Hypothesis (confidence: High). The modal is a UI-complete prototype: state machine, validation copy and success screen are all implemented, but the transport step is absent. The bundle contains no form endpoint, no /api/ path and no third-party form SDK, and the submit produces exactly zero requests — so there is no code path that could deliver the payload.

Evidence
98 requests before submit, 98 after, none during; localStorage/sessionStorage empty; bundle scan: fetch(×1, 0 /api/ paths, no formspree/axios/supabase/calendly/emailjs/netlify/getform/web3forms; <form> has no action and no method.
To confirm
Read the submit handler in the source (search the bundle for the success-state string "Consultation Request Received") and confirm no fetch/XMLHttpRequest call precedes the state transition; then check the intended destination (CRM, email service, or serverless function) exists.
H2 · Anchor IDs were renamed during a section refactor and the links were not updated
Medium
Hypothesis (confidence: Medium, inferred). The live document exposes a short, generic ID set (service, process, case, pricing, blog) while the links reference a richer taxonomy (solutions, architecture, benefits, contact, about, featured-video). That pattern is consistent with sections being consolidated and re-ID'd while the footer/header link map kept the old names. The mechanism is inferred from the ID mismatch, not observed in source control.

Evidence
17 of 25 anchors resolve to null; the 5 working anchors all match the generic ID set; clicking a dead anchor sets the hash but scrolls 0 px.
To confirm
Diff the section components' id attributes against the nav link map in the repo; or add the missing IDs and re-run the anchor resolution check.
H3 · The SPA fallback rewrites every path to index.html, and static crawl assets were never added
High
Hypothesis (confidence: High). A catch-all rewrite serves the 1,571-byte shell for any path, which is why /nonexistent-abc returns 200 rather than 404. Separately, robots.txt, sitemap.xml and favicon.ico return 404 with a 9-byte body — the host answers, the files simply do not exist.

Evidence
Six distinct paths all return HTTP 200 with byte-identical 1,571 B bodies; the three crawl assets return 404 with 9-byte bodies.
To confirm
Inspect the host's rewrite/redirect rules; confirm whether a 404 route exists in the router at all.
H4 · The footer video asset was replaced by a still poster and the source was dropped
Medium
Hypothesis (confidence: Medium, inferred). The element keeps loop, playsinline and preload="metadata" — all video-playback intent — but has no src and no <source>, while a 5.3 MB poster is present and loads. That is consistent with the video file being removed or never uploaded while the poster was kept, leaving a heavy still image behind a dead player.

Evidence
<video class="footer__video" poster="…" loop playsinline preload="metadata"></video> — 0 <source> children, readyState 0, networkState 0; poster returns HTTP 200 at 5,570,213 B.
To confirm
Check the asset manifest / CDN for the intended .mp4/.webm; if it is intentionally a still, replace the <video> with an <img> and compress the poster.
